\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A modular site and a sign loop}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{On one vertex without edges the global action is the full vertex
gauge action. In \ref{fdip-00ZZ} the #{GL_2(\mathbb F_2)} orbit
has three nonzero vectors with sum zero. Their unit weights sum
to #{1} in the field, while #{|G|=0}. Thus the normalized field mean
is zero even though the formula for #{P_{G^X}} in \ref{fdip-010M}
has no denominator inverse. In the sign representation over
#{\mathbb Z/4\mathbb Z}, multiplication by #{1-(-1)=2} instead
annihilates the nonzero fixed element #{2}, as in \ref{fdip-00WN}.}

\p{Over the reals put one sign scalar on one vertex and one sign
link on a loop at that vertex. Take the sign representation on the
real line, and let one face traverse the loop once. Give its two
possible holonomies #{+1,-1} weights #{2,1}, respectively, and
give all other factors weight one. Summing over both scalars gives
link weights #{4,2}. Thus #{Z=6} and the mean link is
#{(4-2)/6=1/3}. On a loop the vertex acts by conjugation, which
fixes both sign holonomies. The independent head rotation in
\ref{fdip-010N} requires distinct endpoints.}
